From: pdp11-emu authors
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] timer: idle rate before calibration, throttle changes at run time

Two faults of the same calibration code: the idle rate taken from a clock
that has not calibrated yet (the UNIX V5 clock stall), and run-time switches
between SET THROTTLE 1M and SET NOTHROTTLE plus SET CPU IDLE (a front end's
speed setting).

1. The idle rate before the first calibration

sim_idle() turns the instructions until the next event into a sleep of
sim_interval / sim_idle_cyc_ms milliseconds.  Only sim_rtcn_calb() measures
sim_idle_cyc_ms, from a calibration second with less than 1% idle; until
then the first sim_idle() call derives it from the calibrated timer's tick
size.  A guest that executes WAIT before its clock completed one calibration
second (UNIX V5 sits at "login:" a few milliseconds after the kernel starts
the KW11-L) freezes the device's initial tick size into it: 16667 cycles per
60Hz tick (1 MIPS), so sim_idle_cyc_ms = 1000 on a host that executes 50-65
million instructions per second, and a mostly idle guest never measures it.

The self-regulating calibration then settles the tick at ~17000 "sleep"
cycles.  In a busy burst (the V5 session's cc) ticks come ~60 times too fast,
a calibration second ends within tens of milliseconds and multiplies the
tick size by up to ~90 (36 observed: 27936 -> 1022068).  Back in WAIT every
tick sleeps ~1 s, so the next calibration second spans 50-60 s and is
discarded as a host suspension ("gap too big", > 30 s): the clock stays at
one tick per second for as long as the guest idles (date advances a second
per minute, tty delays and console polling take seconds per line).

When sim_idle_cyc_ms is still unset, use the start-up precalibrated execution
rate instead of the tick size (simulators without precalibration commands
keep the old derivation).  The sub-millisecond waits before the first
calibration then spin, as on systems that are busy for their first second,
and that calibration measures sim_idle_cyc_ms.  Systems whose first
calibration second is busy (UNIX V6 and V7, both 2.11BSDs, RSX-11M+, RSTS/E;
RT-11 does not idle) never reach this code, nor do SET CPU IDLE=n (stability
wait) and throttling (idling off).

That calibration second has to measure the run alone.  BOOT, GO and RUN zero
sim_gtime (), and their device resets set the clocks' calibration start to
the time the simulator last stopped (at start-up: before the 100 ms
precalibration), but sim_start_timer_services() moved it by the time stopped
only when sim_gtime () was not 0.  The first calibration second after a boot
therefore included the time at the prompt.  A guest that starts the KW11-L
without resetting it while running (UNIX V5's kernel resets it; a bare-metal
program does not) then idled at a rate measured that many times too low:
5442 instead of about 110000 cycles/ms when started 0.3 s after start-up (a
short burst then slowed its clock to 12 ticks/s for 5 s), and started a
second after start-up it stalled at one tick per second as above.  The time
stopped now always moves the calibration start.

2. Throttle changes at run time

SET NOTHROTTLE left sim_throt_state at THROTTLE.  A later SET THROTTLE then
resumed the old wait in sim_throt_sched() and never reached the end of a
measurement, where _sim_timer_adjust_cal() sets the clocks' tick size to the
throttled rate.  The KW11-L kept the tick size calibrated while unthrottled
(1.9 million instructions per 60Hz tick), now executed at 1 MIPS: it ticked
every two seconds, and each calibration second took two minutes and was
discarded as "gap too big", so the clock stalled for good.  Every new
throttle setting (except the fixed N/M form) now starts in INIT and
measures again, as at start-up.  sim_throt_sched() sets the start of that
measurement: with the clock calibrated, INIT goes to TIME at once, and the
old start (0 after a fast boot) averaged over the time at the prompt and
printed a bogus "Host CPU could be too slow" warning.

While throttled, a calibration second with 0% idle (idling is off) measured
sim_idle_cyc_ms at the throttled rate, about 1000 cycles/ms, because the
throttle's sleeps are part of its wall time.  After SET NOTHROTTLE an idle
guest has no further busy second to correct it: the V5 state above, and a
short burst multiplied the tick size and stalled the clock.  sim_rtcn_calb()
no longer measures it in a second in which the throttle slept.

_sim_timer_adjust_cal() forces a calibration on the next tick, which counted
a whole second of virtual time for the ticks of the current second so far;
the catch-up then slowed the clock until the guest had lost up to a second,
at every throttle start.  The forced second now counts only its ticks.

sim_timer.c is part of Open SIMH and carries its X11-style licence
(Copyright (c) Robert M Supnik and the Open SIMH contributors); this patch
is offered under the same terms.
---
diff --git a/sim_timer.c b/sim_timer.c
index 333d26a..9764572 100644
--- a/sim_timer.c
+++ b/sim_timer.c
@@ -188,6 +188,7 @@ static double sim_throt_inst_start;
 static uint32 sim_throt_sleep_time = 0;
 static int32 sim_throt_wait = 0;
 static uint32 sim_throt_delay = 3;
+static t_bool sim_throt_slept = FALSE;              /* throttle slept since the last calibration */
 #define CLK_TPS 100
 #define CLK_INIT (sim_precalibrate_ips/CLK_TPS)
 static int32 sim_int_clk_tps;
@@ -850,6 +851,7 @@ int32 delta_vtime;
 double new_gtime;
 int32 new_currd;
 int32 itmr;
+t_bool throttled;
 RTC *rtc;
 
 if (tmr == SIM_INTERNAL_CLK)
@@ -912,6 +914,8 @@ if (sim_calb_tmr != tmr) {
     return rtc->currd;
     }
 new_rtime = sim_os_msec ();                         /* wall time */
+throttled = sim_throt_slept;                        /* throttle slept this second? */
+sim_throt_slept = FALSE;
 if (!sim_signaled_int_char &&
     ((new_rtime - sim_last_poll_kbd_time) > 500)) {
     sim_debug (DBG_CAL, &sim_timer_dev, "sim_rtcn_calb(tmr=%d) gratuitous keyboard poll after %d msecs\n", tmr, (int)(new_rtime - sim_last_poll_kbd_time));
@@ -970,7 +974,11 @@ if (tmr != SIM_NTIMERS) {
         }
     }
 new_gtime = sim_gtime();
-if ((last_idle_pct == 0) && (delta_rtime != 0)) {
+/* A second with throttle sleeps in its wall time measures the throttled
+   rate (about 1000 cycles/ms at SET THROTTLE 1M), not the one sim_idle
+   needs after SET NOTHROTTLE: an idle guest never has another busy second
+   to correct it, and idle sleeps sized with it stall the clock. */
+if ((last_idle_pct == 0) && (delta_rtime != 0) && !throttled) {
     sim_idle_cyc_ms = (uint32)((new_gtime - rtc->gtime) / delta_rtime);
     if ((sim_idle_rate_ms != 0) && (delta_rtime > 1))
         sim_idle_cyc_sleep = (uint32)((new_gtime - rtc->gtime) / (delta_rtime / sim_idle_rate_ms));
@@ -1601,9 +1609,18 @@ if ((!sim_idle_enab)                             ||     /* idling disabled */
    */
 sim_debug (DBG_TRC, &sim_timer_dev, "sim_idle(tmr=%d, sin_cyc=%d)\n", tmr, sin_cyc);
 if (sim_idle_cyc_ms == 0) {
-    sim_idle_cyc_ms = (rtc->currd * rtc->hz) / 1000;/* cycles per msec */
+    /* No calibration has measured the execution rate yet (only one with
+       less than 1% idle sets sim_idle_cyc_ms), so the timer's tick size may
+       still be its device's initial guess: the PDP-11 KW11-L's is 16667
+       cycles per 60Hz tick (1 MIPS), 50 times too small on current hosts.
+       Frozen here, it makes every idle sleep last many ticks as soon as the
+       tick calibrates to the real rate.  Prefer the precalibrated rate. */
+    uint32 cyc_per_sec = (sim_clock_precalibrate_commands != NULL) ?
+                         (uint32)sim_precalibrate_ips : (uint32)(rtc->currd * rtc->hz);
+
+    sim_idle_cyc_ms = cyc_per_sec / 1000;               /* cycles per msec */
     if (sim_idle_rate_ms != 0)
-        sim_idle_cyc_sleep = (rtc->currd * rtc->hz) / (1000 / sim_idle_rate_ms);/* cycles per minimum sleep */
+        sim_idle_cyc_sleep = cyc_per_sec / (1000 / sim_idle_rate_ms);/* cycles per minimum sleep */
     }
 if ((sim_idle_rate_ms == 0) || (sim_idle_cyc_ms == 0)) {/* not possible? */
     sim_interval -= sin_cyc;
@@ -1753,6 +1770,15 @@ else {
         sim_throt_state = SIM_THROT_STATE_THROTTLE;         /* force state */
         sim_throt_wait = sim_throt_val;
         }
+    else {
+        /* Measure again, as at start-up: only the end of a measurement
+           sets the clocks' tick size to the throttled rate (sim_throt_svc,
+           _sim_timer_adjust_cal).  Resuming an earlier THROTTLE state, as
+           after SET NOTHROTTLE, would keep the tick size calibrated while
+           unthrottled, ~100 times too long: the clock would stall, every
+           calibration second discarded as "gap too big". */
+        sim_throt_state = SIM_THROT_STATE_INIT;
+        }
     }
 if (sim_throt_type == SIM_THROT_SPC) {  /* Set initial value while correct one is determined */
     sim_throt_cps = (int32)((1000.0 * sim_throt_val) / (double)sim_throt_sleep_time);
@@ -1820,6 +1846,8 @@ if (sim_throt_type != SIM_THROT_NONE) {
     else {
         /* Start calibration initially */
         sim_throt_state = SIM_THROT_STATE_INIT;
+        sim_throt_ms_start = sim_os_msec ();            /* a restart with the clock */
+        sim_throt_inst_start = sim_gtime ();            /* calibrated measures from here */
         sim_activate (&sim_throttle_unit, SIM_THROT_WINIT);
         }
     }
@@ -1967,6 +1995,7 @@ switch (sim_throt_state) {
 
     case SIM_THROT_STATE_THROTTLE:                      /* throttling */
         sim_idle_ms_sleep (sim_throt_sleep_time);
+        sim_throt_slept = TRUE;                         /* no idle rate from this second */
         delta_ms = sim_os_msec () - sim_throt_ms_start;
         if (delta_ms >= 10000) {                        /* recompute every 10 sec */
             double delta_insts = sim_gtime() - sim_throt_inst_start;
@@ -2037,6 +2066,7 @@ static void _sim_timer_adjust_cal(void)
 
         if (rtc->hz) {                                      /* running? */
             rtc->currd = (int32)(sim_throt_cps / rtc->hz);/* use throttle calibration */
+            rtc->vtime -= ((rtc->hz - 1 - rtc->ticks) * 1000) / rtc->hz;/* forced second: its ticks only */
             rtc->ticks = rtc->hz - 1;                     /* force clock calibration on next tick */
             rtc->rtime = sim_throt_ms_start - 1000 + 1000/rtc->hz;/* adjust calibration parameters to reflect throttled rate */
             rtc->gtime = sim_throt_inst_start - sim_throt_cps + sim_throt_cps/rtc->hz;
@@ -2541,7 +2571,8 @@ return SCPE_OK;
 void sim_start_timer_services (void)
 {
 int32 tmr;
-uint32 sim_prompt_time = (sim_gtime () > 0) ? (sim_os_msec () - sim_stop_time) : 0;
+uint32 sim_stopped_time = sim_os_msec () - sim_stop_time;
+uint32 sim_prompt_time = (sim_gtime () > 0) ? sim_stopped_time : 0;
 int32 registered_units = 0;
 
 sim_time_at_sim_prompt +=  (((double)sim_prompt_time) / 1000.0);
@@ -2549,11 +2580,16 @@ for (tmr=0; tmr<=SIM_NTIMERS; tmr++) {
     RTC *rtc = &rtcs[tmr];
 
     if (rtc->initd) {                /* calibrated clock initialized? */
-        rtc->rtime += sim_prompt_time;
-        rtc->vtime += sim_prompt_time;
-        sim_debug (DBG_CAL, &sim_timer_dev, "sim_start_timer_services(tmr=%d) - adjusting calibration real time by %d ms\n", tmr, (int)sim_prompt_time);
+        /* The time stopped, also before BOOT, GO and RUN, which zero
+           sim_gtime (): their device resets set a clock's calibration
+           start to sim_stop_time, and its first calibration second would
+           otherwise include the time at the prompt and measure the
+           execution rate (sim_idle_cyc_ms too) that many times too low. */
+        rtc->rtime += sim_stopped_time;
+        rtc->vtime += sim_stopped_time;
+        sim_debug (DBG_CAL, &sim_timer_dev, "sim_start_timer_services(tmr=%d) - adjusting calibration real time by %d ms\n", tmr, (int)sim_stopped_time);
         if (rtc->clock_catchup_eligible)
-            rtc->calib_tick_time += (((double)sim_prompt_time) / 1000.0);
+            rtc->calib_tick_time += (((double)sim_stopped_time) / 1000.0);
         if (rtc->clock_unit)
             ++registered_units;
         }
